@startuml dataflow_example_padded
' Worked example: layout="padded" on a 3x3x4 grid, two variables.
' Every number here is from an actual run of this call.
' Grids: one panel per x0 (= stratum), rows x1 (= lines), columns x2
' (= the padded axis), index space.
' Render: plantuml docs/dataflow_example_padded.puml

title Example 5 -- layout="padded": every line fills its first k cells (experimental)

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Monospaced
skinparam defaultFontSize 12
skinparam ArrowColor #555555
skinparam activity {
  BackgroundColor #FCFCFC
  BorderColor #777777
  DiamondBackgroundColor #F2F2F2
}
skinparam partition {
  BorderColor #8899AA
  FontColor #33495E
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFDF0
  BorderColor #C8C08A
}

start

partition "call" {
  :GenerateData(num_obs=18, num_dims=3, ratio_dims=[3, 3, 4],
                density=[0.5, 0.25], seed=12345,
                num_vars=2, var_dims=3, layout="padded")
  .generate(...);
  note right
    padded_dim defaults to the last dimension, x2.
    Think of x2 as depth and of each (x0, x1) as one
    profile: a profile fills its first k depths and
    the rest of the row is fill, as in Argo.
    overlap must stay "random": under padded it
    follows from the densities (see below).
  end note
}

partition "validate and configure" {
  :nb_coords_dim1 = (18 / (0.5 * 36)) ** (1/3) = 1
  -> <b>shape = [3, 3, 4]</b>, 36 sites;

  :minimum density under padded:
  (prod(shape)/n_padded + n_padded - 1) / prod(shape)
  -> (36/4 + 4 - 1) / 36 = 12/36 = 0.333
  every line needs one observation (9 lines) and one
  line must run the full length (3 more);

  :var_num_obs = 18, 9 (var1 at half var0's density)
  <b>dim_split = 0</b>: the padded axis cannot be split, a
  stratum would hold one cell of each line;
  note right #FDF1F1
    printed: "Stratifying along dimension 0 (size 3)
    rather than the largest dimension 2 (size 4), which
    is not shared by every variable."
    Both variables vary along x2; it is excluded because
    it is the padded axis. The message gives the wrong
    reason.
  end note
}

partition "placement of var0: no LHS stage" {
  :<b>padded_stratum_counts</b>
  lines per stratum = 3 (one per x1), n_padded = 4
  floor = 3 per stratum (one cell per line)
  room  = 3 * (4 - 1) = 9 per stratum
  counts = 3 + apportion(18 - 9, equal weights, cap 9)
      -> 6, 6, 6
  carrier = argmax(counts) = stratum 0
  it needs 4 + 3 - 1 = 6 to run one full line: no deficit;

  :<b>_first_occupied_per_line</b>, per stratum: k per line
  stratum 0 (carrier): line 0 runs the full axis, k = 4,
                       the other lines k = 1   -> 4, 1, 1
  strata 1 and 2: 1 per line, then 3 spare
                  apportioned equally           -> 2, 2, 2;

  :sites are positions 0..k-1 of each line: no random draw
  values from stream(12345, STRATUM, j), after the sites;
  note right
    var0 placed (# = occupied, . = vacant)
           x0=0           x0=1           x0=2
           x2: 0 1 2 3    x2: 0 1 2 3    x2: 0 1 2 3
      x1=0     # # # #        # # . .        # # . .
      x1=1     # . . .        # # . .        # # . .
      x1=2     # . . .        # # . .        # # . .
    Coverage comes from the construction: every line
    holds x2 = 0, and line (0, 0) reaches x2 = 3, so
    every coordinate of every axis is used without an
    LHS stage (which would have put a site off the
    pattern).
  end note
}

partition "placement of var1: the same layout" {
  :<b>counts</b>: apportion(9, 12 cells per stratum) -> 3, 3, 3;

  :var1 has the padded axis and overlap is "random", so
  <b>_padded_cells</b> places it: 3 lines per stratum,
  k = apportion(3, equal weights, cap 4) = 1, 1, 1
  -> x2 = 0 on every line
  values from stream(12345, VAR, 1, j);
  note right
    var1 placed (0 = var0 only, B = both)
           x0=0           x0=1           x0=2
           x2: 0 1 2 3    x2: 0 1 2 3    x2: 0 1 2 3
      x1=0     B 0 0 0        B 0 . .        B 0 . .
      x1=1     B . . .        B 0 . .        B 0 . .
      x1=2     B . . .        B 0 . .        B 0 . .
    Unlike var0, var1 has no coverage guarantee:
    x2 = 1, 2, 3 hold no var1 value.
  end note

  :<b>OverlapCalculator.compute_overlap_report</b>
  overlap   = 9 / 18 = <b>0.500</b> = n1 / n0
  overlap_r = 9 / 9  = <b>1.000</b>;
  note right
    Both variables fill the first cells of the same
    lines, so per line the intersection is min(k0, k1)
    and the overlap is the ratio of the counts. A
    target could not be honoured, which is why passing
    overlap with layout="padded" raises.
  end note
}

partition "two in-memory stores" {
  :<b>NetCDFBuilder.build_dataset</b>
  var0, var1 each (x0, x1, x2) 3x3x4: 72 values, 45 NaN
  attrs: var_num_obs=[18, 9], overlap_target="random",
  overlap_actual=[0.5], overlap_actual_reverse=[1.0];

  :<b>ParquetBuilder.build_multi_var_dataframe</b>
  18 rows, the var0 sites; var1 has no site of its own;
  note right
    DatasetDescription of the written file:
    |= var |= var_sites |= occupied |= density |= overlap |= unused_coords |
    | var0 | 36 | 18 | 0.50 | NaN | 0 |
    | var1 | 36 | 9 | 0.25 | 0.5 | 3 |
  end note
}

partition "disk" {
  :netCDF/ds.nc                13681 B
  parquet/ddf_0.parquet         4653 B
  parquet/_metadata             3765 B
  parquet/_common_metadata      3025 B;
}

:return (Dataset, DataFrame);
stop

@enduml
